ADR-0016
title: Scene beats batch narration and reserved action sequences
status: Accepted
date: 2026-08-16
decision_owner: Product owner
related:
  - ADR-0001
  - ADR-0006
  - ADR-0007
  - docs/current-battle-pipeline.md
  - docs/battle-pacing-candidate-12-v2.md
authority: this file is the causal record; 0016-scene-beats-batched-narration.md is the projection

PROBLEM P1
  Players see little scene movement. One combat turn resolves about one
  action per side, then the narrator describes that small delta. The
  物語 pane therefore ticks in small, similar beats even when
  candidate-12-v2 already shortens the fight and raises damage.

EVIDENCE E1
  Production pacing is candidate-12-v2: turnLimit 12, damage 1.4.
  That changes how fast HP ends, not how far the world or positions
  move in one public story block.

EVIDENCE E2
  A normal combat advance already calls several lightweight providers:
  later-actor decideCharacterAction, semantic/world reconcile,
  expression agents A and B, then a separate narration job.
  Prologue and aftermath add more. Doubling combat turns would
  roughly double those calls.

EVIDENCE E3
  ADR-0006 binds one public narration snapshot to one committed phase
  receipt. Combat receipts are one-per-combat-turn. The UI renders
  one 物語 block per receipt.

EVIDENCE E4
  Expression already proposes a reserved nextAction consumed on a
  later turn. The later actor may still be asked again when the
  first bucket has new facts. There is no reserved sequence of
  length greater than one.

CAUSE C1
  Scene motion is limited by public beat size, not only by per-action
  coefficient size. The story cannot show a burst of movement unless
  several committed micro-turns are allowed to accumulate before
  narration and the next agent call.

REJECTED O1: only raise per-action damage or scene coefficients
  Makes hits larger. Does not create more object, position, or
  causal steps inside one story block.

REJECTED O2: raise turnLimit and keep 1:1 narration plus agents
  More motion, but LLM cost and latency scale with turn count.
  Violates the "do not explode provider calls" driver.

REJECTED O3: let the narrator invent larger movement than the engine
  committed. Rejected by ADR-0006 and ADR-0009: prose has no
  mechanical authority.

REJECTED O4: true simultaneous multi-action in one bucket
  Conflicts with ADR-0001 sequential cause-and-observation order.

DECISION O5: scene beats
  Keep sequential micro-turns as the mechanical unit. Group them
  into a scene beat for agent expression and narration. A beat
  consumes a short reserved action sequence. Close the beat on a
  bounded count, a deterministic world/semantic delta, or a
  terminal event. One lightweight narration job describes the
  whole closed beat.

OWNER_ACCEPTANCE
  Product owner directed merge of this ADR with the log-container
  fix on 2026-08-16 after the Proposed text was presented, and
  ordered implementation as a follow-up branch.

ACCEPTANCE
  ADR-0016 revision dated 2026-08-16 is Accepted. Selected option
  is O5. Rules R1 through R11 are in force.

RULES
  R1. Micro-turn remains the engine unit. ADR-0001 order, bucket
      commits, and receipts stay per micro-turn. Existing battles
      keep one narration per combat receipt.
  R2. A scene beat is a consecutive run of combat micro-turns in
      one battle. Prologue, judgment, and aftermath stay their
      own beats.
  R3. A beat closes on the first of: K committed combat
      micro-turns (first slice K=3), a deterministic scene-delta
      threshold, KO/terminal, or infeasible reserved action.
      K is frozen on the battle with the pacing policy.
  R4. Expression agents run at beat open (or after close, for
      the next beat) and may reserve up to K next actions per
      side. The engine consumes that sequence across micro-turns
      without another expression call while the beat is open.
  R5. decideCharacterAction is skipped when the reserved action
      for that side is still feasible. It is called only when
      the reservation is empty or the server rejects it.
  R6. Semantic/world LLM reconcile runs once when the beat
      closes. Intermediate micro-turns apply only deterministic
      engine and already-accepted world transitions. A later
      actor in the same micro-turn still sees committed earlier
      facts from that micro-turn.
  R7. Narration job creation moves from every combat receipt to
      beat close. The job snapshot includes every micro-turn
      receipt in the beat, in order. Public 物語 shows one
      block per beat, not per micro-turn.
  R8. Observability and /internal/observations still expose
      every micro-turn receipt and pipeline DAG. Beats do not
      hide mechanics.
  R9. First slice must not raise the per-battle lightweight
      provider count above the current 12-turn mean. Target is
      at most one expression pair, one world reconcile, and one
      narration per beat, plus decideCharacterAction only on
      reservation miss.
  R10. Auto-advance HTTP may still be one micro-turn per call.
       Beat close, not HTTP grouping, creates the narration job.
       A later slice may resolve a whole remaining beat inside
       one advance if lease and timeout allow.
  R11. Environment proposeHappening and referee stay at their
       current cadence. This ADR does not add engine-tier calls.

CONSEQUENCES
  + A story block can cover several committed movements.
  + Reserved sequences give "several actions at once" without
    simultaneous buckets.
  + Provider calls grow with beats, not micro-turns.
  - Open beats delay public prose until close.
  - Stale reserved actions need a cheap feasibility check.
  - ADR-0006's one-combat-receipt-one-narration grouping is
    amended for new battles only.

COMPATIBILITY
  Battles created before this ADR keep 1:1 combat narration.
  New battles bind beat K and the beat-close rule at creation.
  No database migration if beat state fits existing receipts
  plus battle-scoped JSON. If a new table is required, it is
  additive and named in the implementation commit.

VERIFICATION after acceptance
  - A three-micro-turn beat with feasible reservations makes
    one narration job and at most one expression pair.
  - A KO on micro-turn 1 closes the beat immediately.
  - Public 物語 has one block per beat; receipts still list
    each micro-turn.
  - Provider ledger counts do not grow linearly with K.
  - Old battles still narrate each combat receipt.
